+# S2: small Rust modules
+
+**The standard.** From [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md): "Target Rust modules under 500 LoC, excluding
+tests. If a file exceeds roughly 800 LoC, add new functionality in a new module instead of extending
+the existing file."
+
+**What is enforced.** A *violation* is a `.rs` file under `codex-rs/` with more than **700 lines**
+(stricter than AGENTS.md's "roughly 800": the team tightened it on 2026-11-21, see *History*)
+(every line of the file counts, blank and comment lines included). 500 is the target and is not
+enforced. Test files are exempt: any file in a `tests/` directory, files named `tests.rs`, and files
+ending in `_tests.rs`.
+
+**The ratchet.** Existing violations are recorded in `.toolbox/results.json`. A pull request fails when:
+- a file already over 700 lines grows, by even one line;
+- a file at or under 700 lines crosses 700;
+- a new (or renamed) file arrives with more than 700 lines.
+
+Shrinking a violating file, or bringing it to 700 or below, passes and is reported as an
+improvement, so the baseline can be tightened.
+
+All commands run from the repository root and need [Toolbox](https://github.com/loganvolkers/toolbox)
+0.1.1 (`toolbox` and `toolbox-connector-runner` on `PATH`), `python3`, `git` and `jq`.
+
+## Report: every violating file, as JSON
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox -o json | jq '[.scores["small-rust-modules"] | to_entries[] | select(.value.rules["at-most-700-lines"] == "fail") | {file: (.key | ltrimstr("rust-file:")), lines: (.value.evidence["at-most-700-lines"][0] | split(" ")[0] | tonumber)}]'
+```
+
+Prints `[{"file": "codex-rs/…", "lines": 1070}, …]`. For the human-readable version (every file
+with its line count and the fix), run `toolbox report .toolbox`.
+
+## Gate: fail when the ratchet breaks
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --out /dev/null
+```
+
+Exit 0 when nothing got worse, 1 on a regression, 2 when Toolbox can't run. It prints one line per
+change, for example:
+
+```
+✗ worse   small-rust-modules · rust-file:codex-rs/analytics/src/events.rs: at-most-700-lines evidence grew 371 → 372
+✗ worse   small-rust-modules · rust-file:codex-rs/analytics/src/facts.rs: at-most-700-lines now fails
+✗ worse   small-rust-modules · rust-file:codex-rs/analytics/src/huge.rs: new, at none; new entities must reach bronze
+✓ better  small-rust-modules · rust-file:codex-rs/analytics/src/reducer.rs: at-most-700-lines evidence shrank 1958 → 1957
+✓ better  small-rust-modules · rust-file:codex-rs/windows-sandbox-rs/src/lib.rs: at-most-700-lines now passes
+```
+
+The evidence count is the number of lines over 700, plus one. Add `--verdict verdict.json` for a
+machine-readable verdict (`regressions`, `improvements`, `changes`). Add `--ci` to also fail when
+`results.json` is out of date (unrecorded improvements or new files).
+
+CI: [`.github/workflows/toolbox-standards.yml`](../.github/workflows/toolbox-standards.yml) runs
+the same gate (`mode: check`) on every pull request, through Toolbox's `actions/report` action. The
+job summary lists regressions and improvements.
+
+## Campaign: progress toward zero violating files
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox -o web --out "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/codex-standards" > /dev/null && jq '.campaigns["small-rust-modules-zero"] | {campaign: .title, status, baseline: .inScope, current: (.inScope - .meeting), percentDone: .percent, percentExact: (if .inScope > 0 then (.meeting * 1000 / .inScope | floor) / 10 else 0 end), targetPercent}' "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/codex-standards/report.json"
+```
+
+`baseline` is the number of files that violated when the campaign started (2026-11-21), `current`
+how many of them still do, and `percentDone` the share fixed (Toolbox rounds down; `percentExact` has
+one decimal). The campaign's scope is frozen in [`campaign.yaml`](./campaign.yaml), so it measures
+paying down the old violations; the ratchet stops new ones. The HTML report
+(`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/codex-standards/index.html`) shows the same progress in its Campaigns tab.
+
+If a file in the campaign is deleted or renamed, it leaves the campaign's scope (the baseline count
+drops by one) instead of counting as done. When you split a file, keep the original path as the
+module root (`mod a; mod b;`) where you can.
+
+## Update the baseline
+
+After a change that shrinks or fixes files (the gate printed `✓ better`), or adds new files:
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --update --out /dev/null
+git add .toolbox/results.json
+```
+
+`--update` refuses to write while anything regressed, so it can only tighten the bar. Never hand-edit
+or hand-merge `results.json`: on a merge conflict, take either side and rerun `--update` on the merged
+tree.
+
+Renaming or moving a file over 700 lines is a regression: to the ratchet it is a new file over the
+limit. Split it below 700 in the same PR, or, if a maintainer approves, reset the baseline: delete
+`.toolbox/results.json`, rerun `--update`, and review the diff (it must show only the renamed
+path). Toolbox has no way to accept one named regression.
+
+## Change the standard
+
+Everything lives in [`toolbox.yaml`](./toolbox.yaml) (the `small-rust-modules` scorecard) and
+[`campaign.yaml`](./campaign.yaml). Check your change with `toolbox validate .toolbox`, then run the
+Gate and update the baseline.
+
+| Change | Where |
+|---|---|
+| **Threshold** (e.g. 700 → 600) | In `toolbox.yaml`, replace `700` in the rule's name (`at-most-700-lines`), `check` (`entity.lines <= 700`), `evidence` (`n > 700` and the `limit 700` label) and `fix`; the same in `campaign.yaml`, and in the Report command above and the workflow's header comment. A lower threshold makes passing files fail (and the `update` refuses), so reset the baseline: delete `.toolbox/results.json`, rerun `--update`, and review the diff (only evidence counts, the rule name and newly failing files should change). Then decide the campaign's scope (see *History*)
+| **Exempt a file** | Add its path to the list at the end of `appliesTo` in `toolbox.yaml`: `entity.path in ['codex-rs/foo/src/generated.rs']`. The file is then skipped; the gate reports it as `✓ better … now passes`; run `--update` |
+| **Exempt a pattern** | Add a condition to `appliesTo`, e.g. `entity.path.startsWith('codex-rs/protocol/src/generated/')` |
+| **Which files are measured** | `.toolbox/rust-file-lines.py` (every tracked or untracked, non-ignored `codex-rs/**/*.rs`). It only measures; the rule and exemptions stay in the manifest |
+| **Campaign scope or dates** | `campaign.yaml`: the frozen file list, `start`, optional `end`, `targetPercent` and `milestones` |
+
+## How it works
+
+| File | Role |
+|---|---|
+| `toolbox.yaml` | The repo portal: a `runner` connector, the `RustFile` kind, the `small-rust-modules` scorecard and its ratchet floor (`newEntitiesAtLeast: bronze`) |
+| `rust-file-lines.py` | Emits one record per Rust file: path, line count, and line numbers (so each line over 700 is one evidence item, and the ratchet sees growth of one line) |
+| `campaign.yaml` | The campaign and its scorecard over the 229 files that violated the 700-line limit on 2026-11-21 |
+| `results.json` | The committed baseline (generated; do not edit) |
+
+## History
+
+**2026-11-21: threshold 800 → 700 lines.** The rule was renamed `at-most-800-lines` →
+`at-most-700-lines` and the baseline reset (deleted and regenerated with `--update`): it now records
+229 files over 700 lines (191 were over 800; 38 are between 701 and 800). The ratchet applies at the
+new limit from then on: a recorded file may not grow, an unrecorded file may not cross 700.
+
+The campaign's scope was re-frozen to those 229 files, keeping `start: 2026-11-21`. Why: the campaign
+had started the same day and no file had been fixed (0 of 191), so nothing was lost; keeping the
+old 191-file scope would have left the 38 newly violating files out of the paydown, and measuring
+the old scope against the new limit would have made the goal ("zero files over 700") differ from
+what the campaign counts. A later threshold change mid-campaign should instead either keep the
+scope and say so here, or end the campaign (`end`) and start a new one, so that progress already
+made stays visible.
